  "format": "Van Lathan joins for a spoiler-heavy 'Brand New Day,' then the three rank all eleven Spider-Man movies, animated ones included.",
  "segments": [
    {
      "kind": "discussion",
      "heading": "Movie News",
      "note": "The Thomas Crown Affair trailer + a Man of Tomorrow tangent",
      "films": [
        {
          "title": "The Thomas Crown Affair",
          "year": 2026,
          "note": "Michael B. Jordan directs and stars in the new remake opposite Adria Arjona; Sean frames it as his 'drop your nuts' movie-star swing, closest he'll get to Bond."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Thomas Crown Affair",
          "year": 1999,
          "note": "Amanda's foundational text: McTiernan, Brosnan, Rene Russo. 'A remake, a little cheesy in just the right way.'"
        },
        {
          "title": "The Thomas Crown Affair",
          "year": 1968,
          "note": "The original; this makes the new one a reimagining 'twice before.'"
        },
        {
          "title": "Man of Tomorrow",
          "year": 2027,
          "note": "Teased near the end re: Arjona possibly playing Wonder Woman and Nicholas Hoult's Luthor war-armor looking a lot like Buzz Lightyear."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "review",
      "heading": "‘Spider-Man: Brand New Day’",
      "note": "with Van Lathan · spoilers throughout",
      "films": [
        {
          "title": "Spider-Man: Brand New Day",
          "year": 2026,
          "note": "Destin Daniel Cretton's fourth Holland Spider-film. Van loved it, Amanda's least favorite of the four, Sean in the middle. It's overloaded: Jon Bernthal's PG-13 Punisher, Ruffalo's Hulk, and Sadie Sink's Jean Grey, who takes over for about 40 minutes. Praised as the first faithful, grounded, solo Peter; dinged for a villain-shaped hole and a dour tone. Sean clocks debts to The Dark Knight and The Matrix."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "ranking",
      "heading": "The Spider-Man Movie Rankings",
      "note": "all eleven, best first",
      "subject": "Spider-Man",
      "ranked": true,
      "films": [
        {
          "title": "Spider-Man 2",
          "year": 2004,
          "note": "No. 1. The Raimi peak; Sean and Amanda hold the line, elite genre filmmaking with the surgery-room Doc Ock horror. Van won't switch it."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse",
          "year": 2018,
          "note": "Van's real No. 1, a 5-star movie for him, the thing that gave him the Todd McFarlane-comic feeling. Letterboxd 4.4."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spider-Man",
          "year": 2002,
          "note": "Raimi's first; Sean slightly prefers it to 2 for the Green Goblin, and flags it as the movie that tipped Hollywood toward franchise city."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse",
          "year": 2023,
          "note": "Busy on rewatch, but the animation, music and scope carry it."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spider-Man: No Way Home",
          "year": 2021,
          "note": "Highs (the Garfield save) they say no future Spider-film can match, but flawed, with an ugly CGI finale."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spider-Man: Homecoming",
          "year": 2017,
          "note": "The Keaton door reveal; a great reinvention. Amanda's on-ramp Spider-Man."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spider-Man: Brand New Day",
          "year": 2026,
          "note": "No. 7 on the group card. Van disagrees; he has it third."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spider-Man: Far From Home",
          "year": 2019,
          "note": "Least of the Holland trilogy; Amanda charmed by the Europe of it all, Sean a little lower."
        },
        {
          "title": "Spider-Man 3",
          "year": 2007,
          "note": "Baggy and wonky but riding the fumes of 1 and 2; better than remembered on rewatch."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Amazing Spider-Man",
          "year": 2012,
          "note": "R.I.P. Garfield; liked but not loved, a coin flip with Spider-Man 3."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Amazing Spider-Man 2",
          "year": 2014,
          "note": "Last. 'Not really arguable,' the fiasco that torpedoed Garfield's run and Marc Webb's career and pushed Sony toward the MCU deal."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
